\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Documents,\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"Board Meetings.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    *   The private section can utilize a dashboard layout that is visually distinct from the more informational layout of the public pages.\\\\n\\\\n**4.2. Responsive Contact Form**\\\\n\\\\nThe contact form will be a primary point of interaction and must be flawless on all devices.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Layout:** It will use a single-column layout on mobile screens for maximum readability and ease of use. On larger screens (tablets and desktops), it may expand to a two-column layout for more efficient use of space.\\\\n*   **Fields:** Standard fields will include Name (required), Email (required, validated), Subject (required), and Message (required).\\\\n*   **User Feedback:**\\\\n    *   **Validation:** Inline validation will provide immediate feedback as the user types (e.g., \\\\\\\"Please enter a valid email address\\\\\\\").\\\\n    *   **Submission:** The \\\\\\\"Submit\\\\\\\" button will show a loading state to prevent double-clicks. Upon completion, a clear success or error message will be displayed without requiring a page reload.\\\\n\\\\n**4.3. Overall User Experience (UX) Considerations**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Simplicity:** The primary audience may have varying levels of technical proficiency. The design must be clean, with clear navigation, legible fonts, and unambiguous calls-to-action.\\\\n*   **Accessibility:** The site will be built to meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards, ensuring it is usable by people with disabilities. This includes proper color contrast, keyboard navigation, and screen-reader compatibility.\\\\n*   **Performance:** Fast load times are essential. Using Next.js for static site generation (SSG) on public pages will ensure a near-instantaneous initial loading experience.\\\\n\\\\n**5. Framework Evaluation**\\\\n\\\\n| Framework Approach | Pros | Cons |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| **React (via Next.js)** | **Best of both worlds:** Fast static pages for public content, dynamic SPA-like experience for the private portal. Huge ecosystem and talent pool. Excellent tooling and deployment options (Vercel). | Higher initial complexity than a simple MPA. Requires JavaScript for core functionality. |\\\\n| **Vue (via Nuxt.js)** | Very similar benefits to Next.js. Often considered to have a gentler learning curve. Excellent documentation. | Smaller ecosystem and community compared to React. |\\\\n| **Multi-Page Application (MPA)**<br/>*(e.g., Astro, WordPress)* | Simple architecture, inherently good for SEO. Each page is independent. Can function with minimal client-side JavaScript. | \\\\\\\"Clunky\\\\\\\" user experience with full-page reloads for every interaction. Managing state (like login status) across pages is less elegant. The private portal would feel less like a modern web application. |\\\\n\\\\n**Recommendation:** **Next.js (React)** is the superior choice for this project. It directly solves the core challenge of serving distinct public (static) and private (dynamic) sections within a single, cohesive application. Its performance and modern user experience capabilities justify the slight increase in complexity over a traditional MPA.\\\\n\\\\n**6. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nBy adopting the proposed Next.js technology stack and adhering to the user-centric UI/UX strategy, we will deliver a high-quality, performant, and easy-to-use website for the HOA. This approach will effectively serve both the public and the association's members, modernizing the HOA's digital presence.\\\\n\\\\nI am prepared to assemble the frontend team and begin architecting the initial project structure upon your approval.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen, your previous analysis task is now obsolete. New Directive: The project architecture has been decided as Next.js (frontend) and Strapi (backend). Your new task is to conduct a deep-dive analysis and produce a detailed technical specification for the frontend. Your report must include: 1. A detailed component breakdown for the Next.js application (e.g., LoginForm, DocumentList, NewsArticle, ProfileEditor). 2. For each key component, specify its purpose, required props, and its internal state management strategy. This specification is critical for the final DEP. Submit your report upon completion.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThis report provides a detailed technical specification for the Next.js frontend application, in accordance with directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02. The previous analysis is considered obsolete. This specification is based on the decided architecture of a Next.js frontend consuming a Strapi backend API. The document outlines a comprehensive component breakdown and provides detailed specifications for key components, covering their purpose, props, and state management. This document will serve as the foundational blueprint for frontend development.\\\\n\\\\n**2. Overall Application Structure**\\\\n\\\\nThe Next.js application will utilize the App Router paradigm. Pages will be organized into two main groups:\\\\n\\\\n*   **/app/(public):** Contains public-facing routes like `/`, `/news`, `/contact`. These pages will be statically generated (SSG) where possible for maximum performance and SEO.\\\\n*   **/app/(private):** Contains member-only routes like `/dashboard`, `/documents`, `/profile`. These routes will be protected by an authentication guard and will be dynamically rendered to display user-specific data.\\\\n\\\\nData fetching will primarily be handled by a combination of Server Components for initial page loads and the SWR (Stale-While-Revalidate) library on the client for dynamic data that needs to be kept fresh.\\\\n\\\\n**3. Component Breakdown & Specification**\\\\n\\\\nBelow is a detailed specification for key components required for the application.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.1. Authentication Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<LoginForm />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To provide an interface for users to enter their credentials and authenticate with the backend. It will handle form input, validation, and submission state.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `onLoginSuccess` | `() => void` | Callback function executed after a successful login, typically to trigger a route change. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `email` | `string` | Stores the value of the email input field. |\\\\n    | `password` | `string` | Stores the value of the password input field. |\\\\n    | `isLoading` | `boolean` | Tracks the submission status to show a loading indicator on the submit button. |\\\\n    | `error` | `string \\\\| null` | Stores any error message returned from the API to display to the user. |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.2. Public-Facing Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<NewsArticle />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A presentational component designed to display the content of a single news article. It receives all necessary data as props.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `title` | `string` | The headline of the article. |\\\\n    | `publishDate` | `string` | The publication date (formatted as a string, e.g., 'October 26, 2023'). |\\\\n    | `authorName` | `string` | The name of the author. |\\\\n    | `contentHtml` | `string` | The body of the article as an HTML string (rendered using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`). |\\\\n    | `imageUrl?` | `string` | *Optional.* URL for a featured image. |\\\\n*   **Internal State:** None. This is a stateless, presentational component.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.3. Member Portal Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<DocumentList />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To fetch and display a filterable and searchable list of HOA documents (e.g., bylaws, meeting minutes) from the Strapi backend. It will manage the state of the fetched data and user filters.\\\\n*   **Props:** None.\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `data`, `error`, `isLoading` | SWR Hook | Managed by SWR to handle fetching documents from the `/api/documents` endpoint. |\\\\n    | `searchTerm` | `string` | Stores the user's input from a search bar to filter documents by title. |\\\\n    | `categoryFilter` | `string` | Stores the selected category (e.g., 'Bylaws', 'Minutes') for filtering. |\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<ProfileEditor />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A form that allows authenticated members to view and update their personal information (e.g., name, phone number, communication preferences). It will pre-populate with existing data and handle the update submission.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `userId` | `string` | The ID of the user whose profile is being edited. Used for API calls. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `initialData` | SWR Hook | Fetches the user's current profile data from `/api/users/{userId}` to populate the form. |\\\\n    | `formData` | `object` | An object holding the state of all form fields (e.g., `{ name: '', phone: '' }`). |\\\\n    | `isSaving` | `boolean` | Tracks the state of the PUT request to the API to disable the form and show a saving indicator. |\\\\n    | `statusMessage` | `object` | Stores a message for the user upon completion (e.g., `{ type: 'success', text: 'Profile saved!' }`). |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**4. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis technical specification provides the core component architecture for the HOA website frontend. By adhering to this plan, the development team can build modular, reusable, and maintainable components. The clear separation of concerns in each component—managing its own state and receiving data via props—will ensure a robust and scalable application architecture. This document is critical for the final DEP and subsequent development sprints.\\\\n\\\\nI await further instructions to proceed with implementation.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"custom\":{}}],\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThis report provides a detailed technical specification for the Next.js frontend application, in accordance with directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02. The previous analysis is considered obsolete. This specification is based on the decided architecture of a Next.js frontend consuming a Strapi backend API. The document outlines a comprehensive component breakdown and provides detailed specifications for key components, covering their purpose, props, and state management. This document will serve as the foundational blueprint for frontend development.\\\\n\\\\n**2. Overall Application Structure**\\\\n\\\\nThe Next.js application will utilize the App Router paradigm. Pages will be organized into two main groups:\\\\n\\\\n*   **/app/(public):** Contains public-facing routes like `/`, `/news`, `/contact`. These pages will be statically generated (SSG) where possible for maximum performance and SEO.\\\\n*   **/app/(private):** Contains member-only routes like `/dashboard`, `/documents`, `/profile`. These routes will be protected by an authentication guard and will be dynamically rendered to display user-specific data.\\\\n\\\\nData fetching will primarily be handled by a combination of Server Components for initial page loads and the SWR (Stale-While-Revalidate) library on the client for dynamic data that needs to be kept fresh.\\\\n\\\\n**3. Component Breakdown & Specification**\\\\n\\\\nBelow is a detailed specification for key components required for the application.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.1. Authentication Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<LoginForm />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To provide an interface for users to enter their credentials and authenticate with the backend. It will handle form input, validation, and submission state.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `onLoginSuccess` | `() => void` | Callback function executed after a successful login, typically to trigger a route change. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `email` | `string` | Stores the value of the email input field. |\\\\n    | `password` | `string` | Stores the value of the password input field. |\\\\n    | `isLoading` | `boolean` | Tracks the submission status to show a loading indicator on the submit button. |\\\\n    | `error` | `string \\\\| null` | Stores any error message returned from the API to display to the user. |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.2. Public-Facing Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<NewsArticle />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A presentational component designed to display the content of a single news article. It receives all necessary data as props.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `title` | `string` | The headline of the article. |\\\\n    | `publishDate` | `string` | The publication date (formatted as a string, e.g., 'October 26, 2023'). |\\\\n    | `authorName` | `string` | The name of the author. |\\\\n    | `contentHtml` | `string` | The body of the article as an HTML string (rendered using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`). |\\\\n    | `imageUrl?` | `string` | *Optional.* URL for a featured image. |\\\\n*   **Internal State:** None. This is a stateless, presentational component.\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n### **3.3. Member Portal Components**\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<DocumentList />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To fetch and display a filterable and searchable list of HOA documents (e.g., bylaws, meeting minutes) from the Strapi backend. It will manage the state of the fetched data and user filters.\\\\n*   **Props:** None.\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `data`, `error`, `isLoading` | SWR Hook | Managed by SWR to handle fetching documents from the `/api/documents` endpoint. |\\\\n    | `searchTerm` | `string` | Stores the user's input from a search bar to filter documents by title. |\\\\n    | `categoryFilter` | `string` | Stores the selected category (e.g., 'Bylaws', 'Minutes') for filtering. |\\\\n\\\\n**Component: `<ProfileEditor />`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A form that allows authenticated members to view and update their personal information (e.g., name, phone number, communication preferences). It will pre-populate with existing data and handle the update submission.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    | Prop | Type | Description |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `userId` | `string` | The ID of the user whose profile is being edited. Used for API calls. |\\\\n*   **Internal State (`useState`, SWR):**\\\\n    | State Variable | Type | Purpose |\\\\n    | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n    | `initialData` | SWR Hook | Fetches the user's current profile data from `/api/users/{userId}` to populate the form. |\\\\n    | `formData` | `object` | An object holding the state of all form fields (e.g., `{ name: '', phone: '' }`). |\\\\n    | `isSaving` | `boolean` | Tracks the state of the PUT request to the API to disable the form and show a saving indicator. |\\\\n    | `statusMessage` | `object` | Stores a message for the user upon completion (e.g., `{ type: 'success', text: 'Profile saved!' }`). |\\\\n\\\\n--- \\\\n\\\\n**4. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis technical specification provides the core component architecture for the HOA website frontend. By adhering to this plan, the development team can build modular, reusable, and maintainable components. The clear separation of concerns in each component—managing its own state and receiving data via props—will ensure a robust and scalable application architecture. This document is critical for the final DEP and subsequent development sprints.\\\\n\\\\nI await further instructions to proceed with implementation.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\"}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"finishReason\":\"STOP\",\"index\":0}],\"usageMetadata\":{\"promptTokenCount\":4058,\"candidatesTokenCount\":1602,\"totalTokenCount\":7100,\"promptTokensDetails\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"tokenCount\":4058}],\"thoughtsTokenCount\":1440},\"modelVersion\":\"models/gemini-2.5-pro\",\"responseId\":\"4bBhaMrLH_Cn1MkPubOOKA\"},\"usage\":{\"inputCharacters\":17224,\"inputImages\":0,\"inputVideos\":0,\"inputAudioFiles\":0,\"outputCharacters\":6037,\"outputImages\":0,\"outputVideos\":0,\"outputAudioFiles\":0,\"inputTokens\":4058,\"outputTokens\":1602,\"totalTokens\":7100},\"latencyMs\":32748.513999999966}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"googleai/gemini-2.5-pro-preview-03-25","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}},"03565989948133b1":{"spanId":"03565989948133b1","traceId":"176f6f014314fd817362624efb2e4b96","startTime":1751232704823,"endTime":1751232737573.3433,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"util","genkit:name":"generate","genkit:isRoot":true,"genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}","genkit:input":"{\"model\":\"googleai/gemini-2.5-pro-preview-03-25\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"TODO: EMERGENCY PROMPT NOTICE: You are an agent of the user. There has been a catastrophic system failure. Your primary role is to assist the user in their tasks.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), you are tasked with the frontend technical analysis for the HOA website. Propose a technology stack and UI/UX strategy. Your analysis must address: 1) How to effectively present distinct public and private sections to the user. 2) A plan for building a responsive contact form. 3) Overall user experience considerations. Evaluate the pros and cons of potential frameworks (e.g., React, Vue, or a multi-page application approach). Submit your findings in a formal report.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CONFIRMATION-KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nDate: [Current Date]\\nSubject: Frontend Technical Analysis and Strategy for HOA Website\\nReference ID: GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-01\\n\\n***\\n\\n### **Formal Report: HOA Website Frontend Technical Analysis**\\n\\n**1. Executive Summary**\\n\\nThis report outlines the proposed frontend technology stack and UI/UX strategy for the new Homeowners Association (HOA) website, as per your directive. The primary recommendation is to use the **Next.js (React) framework**. This choice offers the best combination of performance for public-facing pages and dynamic interactivity for the private member portal. The UI/UX strategy prioritizes simplicity, accessibility, and a clear distinction between public and private sections to ensure an intuitive experience for all users. The plan includes a robust, responsive contact form built for ease of use across all devices.\\n\\n**2. Introduction**\\n\\nThe objective of this analysis is to define a comprehensive frontend strategy that addresses the unique needs of an HOA website. This includes serving two distinct audiences (the general public and authenticated HOA members), providing straightforward communication channels, and ensuring a professional and user-friendly experience. This report details the recommended technologies and strategic approaches to achieve these goals.\\n\\n**3. Proposed Technology Stack**\\n\\n*   **Framework:** **Next.js (React)**\\n    *   **Justification:** Next.js provides hybrid static and server rendering. This allows us to build the public-facing pages (Home, About, etc.) as highly-performant, SEO-friendly static pages, while the private, members-only section can be rendered dynamically as a secure, app-like experience. The large React ecosystem ensures access to a wide array of libraries and a large developer talent pool.\\n*   **Styling:** **Tailwind CSS**\\n    *   **Justification:** A utility-first CSS framework that enables rapid development of a custom, responsive design without writing extensive custom CSS. This is ideal for efficiently building components like the responsive contact form and ensuring a consistent look and feel.\\n*   **State Management:** **React Context API / SWR**\\n    *   **Justification:** For managing the logged-in state and fetching member-specific data, React's built-in Context API is lightweight and sufficient for the scale of this project. For data fetching, Vercel's SWR library provides a simple and powerful solution for caching and revalidating data, improving the user experience in the private section.\\n*   **Form Handling:** **React Hook Form**\\n    *   **Justification:** A performant, flexible, and easy-to-use library for managing form state and validation. It minimizes re-renders and simplifies the implementation of complex validation rules for the contact form and any other forms in the private portal (e.g., submitting maintenance requests).\\n*   **Deployment:** **Vercel**\\n    *   **Justification:** As the creators of Next.js, Vercel offers a seamless, optimized, and highly-scalable deployment platform. Its CI/CD pipeline is trivial to set up, enabling rapid iteration and deployment.\\n\\n**4. UI/UX Strategy**\\n\\n**4.1. Public vs. Private Sections**\\n\\nA clear and intuitive separation between public and private content is critical.\\n\\n*   **Authentication Flow:** Non-authenticated users will only see public pages. A clear \\\"Member Login\\\" button will be present in the main navigation. Attempting to access a private URL will redirect the user to the login page.\\n*   **Visual Distinction:** Once a member logs in, the UI will subtly change to reinforce their authenticated status.\\n    *   The \\\"Member Login\\\" button will be replaced with a \\\"Welcome, [Member Name]\\\" message and a \\\"Logout\\\" button.\\n    *   The main navigation will be updated to include private links such as \\\"Pay Dues,\\\" \\\"Member Directory,\\\" \\\"Documents,\\\" and \\\"Board Meetings.\\\"\\n    *   The private section can utilize a dashboard layout that is visually distinct from the more informational layout of the public pages.\\n\\n**4.2. Responsive Contact Form**\\n\\nThe contact form will be a primary point of interaction and must be flawless on all devices.\\n\\n*   **Layout:** It will use a single-column layout on mobile screens for maximum readability and ease of use. On larger screens (tablets and desktops), it may expand to a two-column layout for more efficient use of space.\\n*   **Fields:** Standard fields will include Name (required), Email (required, validated), Subject (required), and Message (required).\\n*   **User Feedback:**\\n    *   **Validation:** Inline validation will provide immediate feedback as the user types (e.g., \\\"Please enter a valid email address\\\").\\n    *   **Submission:** The \\\"Submit\\\" button will show a loading state to prevent double-clicks. Upon completion, a clear success or error message will be displayed without requiring a page reload.\\n\\n**4.3. Overall User Experience (UX) Considerations**\\n\\n*   **Simplicity:** The primary audience may have varying levels of technical proficiency. The design must be clean, with clear navigation, legible fonts, and unambiguous calls-to-action.\\n*   **Accessibility:** The site will be built to meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards, ensuring it is usable by people with disabilities. This includes proper color contrast, keyboard navigation, and screen-reader compatibility.\\n*   **Performance:** Fast load times are essential. Using Next.js for static site generation (SSG) on public pages will ensure a near-instantaneous initial loading experience.\\n\\n**5. Framework Evaluation**\\n\\n| Framework Approach | Pros | Cons |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **React (via Next.js)** | **Best of both worlds:** Fast static pages for public content, dynamic SPA-like experience for the private portal. Huge ecosystem and talent pool. Excellent tooling and deployment options (Vercel). | Higher initial complexity than a simple MPA. Requires JavaScript for core functionality. |\\n| **Vue (via Nuxt.js)** | Very similar benefits to Next.js. Often considered to have a gentler learning curve. Excellent documentation. | Smaller ecosystem and community compared to React. |\\n| **Multi-Page Application (MPA)**<br/>*(e.g., Astro, WordPress)* | Simple architecture, inherently good for SEO. Each page is independent. Can function with minimal client-side JavaScript. | \\\"Clunky\\\" user experience with full-page reloads for every interaction. Managing state (like login status) across pages is less elegant. The private portal would feel less like a modern web application. |\\n\\n**Recommendation:** **Next.js (React)** is the superior choice for this project. It directly solves the core challenge of serving distinct public (static) and private (dynamic) sections within a single, cohesive application. Its performance and modern user experience capabilities justify the slight increase in complexity over a traditional MPA.\\n\\n**6. Conclusion**\\n\\nBy adopting the proposed Next.js technology stack and adhering to the user-centric UI/UX strategy, we will deliver a high-quality, performant, and easy-to-use website for the HOA. This approach will effectively serve both the public and the association's members, modernizing the HOA's digital presence.\\n\\nI am prepared to assemble the frontend team and begin architecting the initial project structure upon your approval.\\n\\nRespectfully,\\n\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\nFrontend Lead\\n\\n###END-OF-REPORT###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8